{
  "_comment": "HAND-WRITTEN corpus (the second one here that is not generated from the browser reference). It is hand-written because the rule it pins is a DECISION, not any one relay's behaviour: before this corpus existed the three relays disagreed three ways on a non-string `metadata.instance_key`, with no majority to defer to, so generating `expect_api_key` from the browser would have dressed one relay up as an oracle for a rule it never owned. THE RULE (invariant 14 in frontend/src/lib/refresh/CLAUDE.md): on the SELECTOR side, absent / null / \"\" are the same selector -- the provider-default lookup; any other value is an exact string lookup. On the ENTRY side, read `metadata.instance_key`; if `metadata` is not an object the key counts as absent. The entry occupies the DEFAULT slot iff that key is absent, null, or \"\". A non-string, non-null value (number, bool, array, object) is a foreign value the format permits but no writer produces: the entry is neither the default card nor any named card, so no lookup returns it -- unreachable, not an error. IDENTITY is asserted through `api_key`, not `id`, because the macOS port's VaultEntry carries no id. SCOPE: the lookup ALONE, over an ALREADY-PARSED document -- every consumer enters at that same layer (the CLI hands `entry_for` a raw dict, macOS hands `entryFor` a raw JSONValue, and the browser casts rather than routing through `deserializeVault`), because validating on one side only would give that port a stricter pre-step than the equality claims. Documents are kept schema-valid anyway: realistic input, and it keeps the stricter harness available. A non-object element inside `entries` is NOT covered here -- the browser's `Vault` type forbids one and its predicate would throw on it, where the other two step over it, so asserting it would fabricate an agreement the three cannot actually reach. Each port covers that case in its own unit tests.",
